  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nUpper level ridge will begin its slow exit to the east out of the \nsouthern Great Lakes region, but still have two days of dry and \nabove normal temperatures in the forecast ahead of the next cold \nfront. Low dewpoints and daytime RH values are the main impacts as \nwe continue through this extended period of precipitation-free \nweather. Temperatures each day will be roughly 12-18F above normal \nfor mid November with highs in the 60s. Sunday could see 60s as \nwell, but the cold front begins to cut through the CWA in the late\nafternoon, driven by an upper level trough diving into the northern \nGreat Lakes and a Pacific origin weaker trough axis moving in from \nthe west. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nAnafrontal precipitation expected as low level frontogenesis moves \ninto the western zones after 00Z Monday, lagging behind the surface \ncold front. Precipitation is likely to be scattered in a line, and \nshould move through the CWA fairly methodically. As a result, \noverall QPF is on the lower side, generally less than a tenth of an \ninch with isolated locations closer to two tenths in the eastern \nCWA. One thing to watch will be a coastal low pressure system that \nwill move up the East Coast with a broad precipitation shield. \nModels still differ a bit on track, and overall it should stay east \nof our CWA, but some solutions offer a rather close proximity of \nthis shield to our area late Sunday, prior to frontal passage. \nBehind the cold front, chillier airmass moves into the southern \nGreat Lakes with Canadian high pressure, and temperatures heading \ninto next week 15-25F cooler than the short term forecast highs.",
